  "question": {
    "text": "On set $A=\\{1,2,3\\},$ relations $R$ and $S$ are given by $R=\\{(1,1),(2,2),(3,3),(1,2),(2,1)\\}$\r\n$S=\\{(1,1),(2,2),(3,3),(1,3),(3,1)\\}$\r\nThen,",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "HznxX",
      "text": "$R \\cup S$ is an equivalence relation",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "VimQT",
      "text": "$R \\cup S$ is reflexive and transitive but not symmetric",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "TbkP3",
      "text": "$R \\cup S$ is reflexive and symmetric but not transitive",
      "isCorrect": true,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "U5NXt",
      "text": "$R \\cup S$ is symmetric and transitive but not reflexive",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "We have, $R=\\{(1,1),(2,2),(3,3),(1,2),(2,1)\\}$<br />$S=\\{(1,1),(2,2),(3,3),(1,3),(3,1)\\}$<br />$\\therefore R \\cup S=\\{(1,1),(2,2),(3,3),(1,2),(2,1),(1,3),(3,1)\\}$<br />Since, $(2,1) \\in R \\cup S,(1,3) \\in R \\cup S$<br />but $(2,3) \\in R \\cup S$<br />$\\therefore \\mathrm{R} \\cup \\mathrm{S}$ is reflexive and symmetric but not transitive.",
    "image": null
